## Handtekeningen en versleuteling

sig-trust = Trust certificate
sig-broken = ⚠ The digital signature does not match. The message was changed after signing, or the signature is forged.
sig-encrypted = 🔒 Encrypted message. Only the recipients can read it; the subject was not encrypted on the way.
sig-pgp-broken = ⚠ The OpenPGP signature does not match. The message was changed after signing, or the signature is forged.
sig-trust-hint = Keeps this certificate for this address: the signature counts as trusted from now on and you can mail back encrypted. Do this only if you are sure it is the sender's, for example because you checked.
sig-pgp-valid = ✔ Signed with the OpenPGP key of { $name } <{ $email }> ({ $key }). The message was not changed on the way.
sig-pgp-other = ⚠ Signed with the OpenPGP key of { $signer }, not of the sender { $from }. Be careful.
sig-pgp-unknown-key = This message is signed with OpenPGP, but the sender's key is not known yet, so Merel cannot check the signature.
sig-pgp-unchecked = This message is signed with OpenPGP, but Merel cannot check the signature: { $reason }.
sig-pgp-fingerprint = Fingerprint { $fingerprint }. Merel got this key with earlier mail from this address (Autocrypt); compare the fingerprint with the sender if you want to be sure.
sig-valid = ✔ Digitally signed by { $name } <{ $email }>. The message was not changed on the way.
sig-untrusted-issuer = ⚠ Signed as { $name } <{ $email }>, but the issuer ({ $issuer }) is not known as trustworthy. Anyone can make such a certificate; trust it only if you know it is this sender's.
unknown = unknown
sig-other = ⚠ Signed with a certificate of { $signer }, not of the sender { $from }. Be careful.
sig-unchecked = This message is digitally signed, but Merel cannot check the signature: { $reason }.
sig-certificate = Certificate of { $name }, issued by { $issuer }, valid until { $until }. Merel does not check online whether it was revoked.

